INT4 on Phoenix, gate C: the chip runs int4 weights, and the engine's own uint8 x int4 pair
==========================================================================================

Desktop 2 (DESKTOP-CBL5NUA; Ryzen 7 8700G, Phoenix XDNA1, Windows 11), 2026-09-23, mlir-aie
v1.4.2 ironenv (Peano llvm-aie 22.0.0), NPU driver 32.0.20101.3760, XRT 2.21.0, NPU firmware
1.5.5.391, power mode Default (all read by the witness) -- the same driver, XRT and toolchain as
the 2026-09-10 W4A8 logs. Branch worktree-int4-study. NPU runs 05:06:25Z-05:15:47Z.

QUESTION
--------
AMD does not support int4 on XDNA1, and main's docs/SILICON.md still listed int8 x int4 MACs
as AIE2p only. With the documentation the repo has, can int4 be demonstrated on this chip, or
killed before any engine work? Gates A (toolchain, int4_isa_gate_desktop2_20260923.log) and B
(engine bytes, int4_engine_bytes_gate_desktop2_20260923.log) are compile- and schedule-only.
This log is gate C, the silicon:
  3a. Does the 2026-09-10 one-core W4A8 probe (int8 x int4) still reproduce on this machine?
      Its branch had been lost from every ref and was restored onto this one from b0d12d2.
  3b. Does uint8 x int4 -- the operand pair the graph engine would need, since its activations
      are uint8 (zero point 128) -- compute right? aie_api declares it; nothing had run it.
  3c. Does the whole-array best-tile result (int4 B 1.23-1.26x int8 at 64/128/64, 2048^3)
      reproduce?

PRE-REGISTERED (the study plan, written before the run)
-------------------------------------------------------
  Gate C kills uint8 x int4 for the engine if it mismatches an int64 reference or will not
  lower. Expected: passes. Check: 0 mismatches reported per arm, clean pre-flight and witness.
  A pilot of the new arm ran first (u8native:unroll2, K=64, 3 timed calls, not in the raw
  JSONL): 0 of 4,096 mismatches, 2,160 cycles per call, IRON object identical to the static
  compile, xrt-smi clean afterwards.

ANSWER
------
1. The demo runs. 3a reproduces 2026-09-10 CYCLE FOR CYCLE: all 27 (kernel, mode, K) cells of
   the one-core probe -- int8 control, int4 unpack, native int8 x int4, 2x2 variants -- give the
   same median trace cycles per call as on 2026-09-10, 54 of 54 processes bit-exact, every IRON
   object identical bundle for bundle to its static compile. The native k loop again fits
   10.674 cycles per unit K: 383.7 MAC/cycle, 1.88x the int8 control (section A, fit below).
2. uint8 x int4 is native and costs nothing extra. aie::mmul<4,16,8,uint8,int4> matched
   sum(uint8 * int4) exactly in all 12 cells (2 arms x 2 modes x K 64/128/256, 2 processes each,
   checked on the first and the last timed call), with 50.0-50.6% of A at or above 128 -- where
   reading A as signed would have changed every product -- and B low nibble first, two's
   complement. Every cell's cycle count equals its int8 twin's from 3a: uint8 A is only the
   MAC's sign bit. The engine's own pair, mmul<4,8,8,uint8,int8>, equals int8 x int8 the same
   way. So the int4 MAC the engine would use exists, is exact, and runs at 1.88x its int8 k loop
   on one core (section B).
3. The array result reproduces and holds its sign: native int4 at 64/128/64 runs 6,206.73 GOPS
   against upstream int8's 4,821.17 in this sitting, 1.287x (2026-09-10: 6,195.33 against
   4,906.23, 1.263x). Run ranges disjoint (native's slowest run 2,804.5 us, upstream's fastest
   3,548.8 us), every run bit-exact, and every run returned C sha256 e426d437887b5f12 -- the
   same hash every 2026-09-10 arm returned (section C).
4. The witness saw at most one hardware context in 562 one-second samples and no sample with
   two processes on the NPU; xrt-smi reported no contexts before and after.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
----------------------------
- Nothing here is a network. It is one core at M = N = 64, K <= 256, and one array GEMM shape
  at one tile. No W4 accuracy has been measured anywhere in this repo.
- Gate C passing does not reopen int4 for the graph engine. Gate B killed it on bytes: its
  fixed 9,472 B weight packets make int4 save nothing, and even with variable-size packets its
  best case is under 5% of dispatch on every container (int4_engine_bytes_gate_desktop2_20260923.log).
  The MAC being faster does not matter where the core is not the critical path.
- The array ratio moved from 1.263x to 1.287x. Upstream int8 averaged 1.7% slower in this
  sitting (4,821.17 against 4,906.23 GOPS), and native int4 0.2% faster. Both moves are inside
  this machine's day-to-day drift, and neither is attributed to anything.
- The one-core cycle identity is a statement about statically scheduled cores running
  byte-identical objects. It is not evidence that wall-clock timing is stable across days.

